    workingAsExpat: 'The UAE job market is almost entirely composed of expats in private sector roles. Most positions require employer sponsorship (work visa) — the employer arranges the visa, Emirates ID, and medical check. The 2021 labour reforms allow easier job changes without previous employer approval. Freelance licences are available through free zones (Dubai Media City, IFZA, etc.) for ~AED 15,000/year. The UAE has no unemployment benefits or pension system for expats — save aggressively. End-of-Service Gratuity (21 days salary per year for first 5 years, 30 days after) is paid on departure.',
    taxConsiderations: 'The UAE has zero personal income tax — this is the single most attractive financial feature. Corporate tax was introduced at 9% in 2023 for profits over AED 375,000, but personal earnings remain untaxed. VAT is 5%. No capital gains tax, no inheritance tax. Foreign nationals should be aware of their home country\'s tax obligations — US citizens must still file US taxes, UK citizens may have UK tax liability on UK-sourced income. Financial planning is essential; build significant savings during UAE years as retirement provisions are entirely individual.',
    bankingAndMoney: 'Emirates NBD, ADCB, FAB (First Abu Dhabi Bank) and ENBD are the main banks. Opening requires Emirates ID, employment visa, and employer salary certificate. Digital banks Liv and Wio are popular with younger expats. Wise is available for international transfers. UAE is increasingly cashless (Apple Pay, card payments everywhere) but cash (Dirhams, AED) is useful for souks and taxis. No tips required but 10% appreciated in restaurants. ATMs are ubiquitous and reliable.',
    localCulture: 'Emirati culture is deeply rooted in Islamic traditions and Bedouin heritage. Arabic is the official language though English is the working language of business and daily life. Respect for Ramadan (no public eating, drinking or smoking during daylight hours) is legally required. Modest dress applies in malls, traditional areas and government buildings. Emirati nationals are a minority in their own country and may seem reserved — genuine interaction with Emiratis is possible but less common than with the multi-national expat community. Friday is the day of prayer (equivalent of Sunday) and the work week is Monday-Friday.',
