    workingAsExpat: 'Georgia\'s 365-day visa-free access (for 95+ nationalities) makes it uniquely flexible for remote workers — enter, stay a year, leave briefly, re-enter. The P.E. (Individual Entrepreneur) status allows foreigners to register a legal business in Georgia with a flat 1% income tax on turnover up to 500,000 GEL (~$180,000). This has attracted enormous numbers of freelancers and remote workers. Local employment is limited and Georgian language is needed for most local roles. The economy is growing but wages are low by Western standards.',
    taxConsiderations: 'Georgia has a flat 20% personal income tax and a flat 20% corporate tax. As an Individual Entrepreneur (IE/SP), tax can be as low as 1% on turnover up to 500,000 GEL. There is no capital gains tax on stocks or cryptocurrency. Georgia has tax residency rules: 183+ days makes you a Georgian tax resident. Virtual Zone status allows certain IT companies to pay 0% on foreign-sourced income. The combination of easy business registration and very low taxes makes Georgia exceptionally attractive for freelancers and online businesses.',
    bankingAndMoney: 'TBC Bank and Bank of Georgia are the two major banks — both have good English apps and services. Account opening is simple: passport, proof of address and sometimes proof of visa status. The Georgian Lari (GEL) is the local currency. USD and EUR are widely accepted and can be exchanged at competitive rates. Wise and Revolut work well. ATMs widely available. Cash is king in many local restaurants and markets. Georgia is affordable: $700-1,200/month covers a comfortable lifestyle.',
    localCulture: 'Georgian culture is extraordinarily hospitable — the word "guest" in Georgian literally means "gift from God." The Supra (feast) tradition involves lavish spreads of food, natural wine and heartfelt toasts. Georgian Orthodox Christianity is deeply embedded in national identity. The ancient Georgian script is visually striking and entirely unique. Georgian polyphonic singing is UNESCO-listed. Political tensions with Russia (since the 2008 war and occupied territories of South Ossetia and Abkhazia) shape political discourse — Georgians feel passionately about their sovereignty. The 2024 elections and "foreign agents law" controversy increased political complexity.',
