    workingAsExpat: 'Singapore\'s Employment Pass (EP) is for professionals earning SGD 5,000+/month; the S Pass is for mid-skilled workers earning SGD 3,150+. The EP application is through the employer and MOM (Ministry of Manpower). Singapore has tightened foreign hiring requirements (COMPASS framework since 2023) — employers must demonstrate they couldn\'t fill the role locally. The Tech.Pass allows established tech professionals to work without a specific job offer. Entrepreneurship: EntrePass allows business founders to set up and operate startups in Singapore.',
    taxConsiderations: 'Singapore\'s income tax is highly competitive: progressive from 0-22%, with effective rates typically 7-12% for most professionals. No capital gains tax. No inheritance tax. Foreign income not remitted to Singapore is generally not taxed. The IRAS (Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore) is efficient and digital. Tax residency kicks in after 183 days. CPF (Central Provident Fund) contributions apply to Singapore citizens and PRs only — not foreign EP holders. Singapore is consistently rated one of the most tax-efficient destinations for high earners.',
    bankingAndMoney: 'DBS, OCBC and UOB are the three local banks — all excellent. HSBC and Citibank have strong presence. Account opening requires EP, passport and proof of address. DBS Digibank is excellent digitally. Wise is widely used for international transfers. Singapore is increasingly cashless — PayNow (instant bank transfer), PayLah (DBS), NETS and card payments are universal. Some hawker centres accept PayNow QR payments. ATMs widely available. Tipping is not customary in Singapore (service charge of 10% is usually added to restaurant bills).',
    localCulture: 'Singapore\'s culture is a unique blend of Malay, Chinese, Indian and British colonial heritage with an overlay of efficient modernity. "Kiasu" (fear of losing out, competitive) is a well-known cultural trait — queuing, studying hard and maximising opportunities. Singlish (Singaporean English creole) adds local flavour — "lah," "leh," "meh" at sentence endings are endearing linguistic markers. Food is the ultimate cultural equaliser — hawker centres are where all races and classes eat together. Public behaviour is highly regulated (no chewing gum, heavy fines for littering). The society is conservative by Western standards but cosmopolitan in practice.',
